in a recent video I described the  benefits of training across modalities  of combining kettle bells gymnastic  rings powerlifting calisthenics animal  movements bodybuilding steady-state  cardio high-intensity interval training  all in a single training program each  method brings different benefits that  can complement the others making this a  superior way to build overall  performance that video got a better  response than I was expecting and it  seems a lot of you out there liked the  approach but a question that came up a  lot in the comments was how surely  combining kettle bells with powerlifting  with high-intensity interval training  will result in overtraining burn out an  injury or at the very least leave you  with no time to do anything else well  actually I don't see the problem the aim  here is not to perform an entire  bodybuilding leg day followed by a  calisthenics leg day followed by a day  of heavy squats or to be buying that all  into a single workout instead we'd  create a leg day or a full-body split by  cherry-picking the best exercises from  each discipline for our goals and of  course you don't have to work with every  one of those modalities either you can  pick just two or three as you like often  that might mean using kettlebells or  bodyweight training as accessories to  improve our strength on the big lifts or  it might mean doing some isolation work  on a leg curl machine to strengthen the  hamstrings at a particular range of  motion or it could mean combining  different exercises heavy squats  followed by pistol squats followed by  Cossack squats followed by weighted  lunge walks followed by kettlebell  swings followed by calf jumps with a  quasi isometric squat to finish this way  we're training max strength with heavy  squats  single leg strength with pistol squats  mobility and movement in the frontal  play with the Cossack squat ballistic  strength muscle endurance and hip hinge  with the kettlebell swings strength  endurance with the lunge walks an  isolated calf strength of the calf jumps  across the isometric squat builds great  control awareness and mobility and  serves as the perfect cool-down  opportunity as well as a way to perform  a moving meditation of course we'll be  doing less of each movement in fact I'll  rarely do three sets of anything but  this is hardly a problem when I'm moving  straight on to another movement the  targets many of the same muscles the key  is to place the most intense compound  and difficult movements first and to  gradually move towards the easier ones  that are more isolated that involves  slower twitch muscle fiber or that may  even be a towards cardio this reduces  injury  and ensures that you're able to perform  the most challenging movements whilst  you're fresh improving gains and  progress it's essentially reverse  pyramid training but where the weight  isn't the only deciding factor it's very  important that you listen to your body  here and we can take this even further  with a mechanical drop set that  incorporates different movements into a  single set moving straight from one  movement to the other as soon as you  reach technical failure for example you  might perform as many bench presses as  you can on a specific weight then  immediately start doing push ups  this provides you with the benefits  opposed to bench press the building max  strength and the push up for building  strength endurance and pump I also like  to vary cadence and intent in a single  set switching from slow quasi isometrics  to plyometrics and everything in between  I call these dynamic drop sets and you  can check one out in my recent video on  a push-up routine this is also a great  way to incorporate strategies to make  common movements more difficult so for  example if you want to train your finger  strength and fingertip push-ups are  ideal of course if you only do fingertip  push-ups then you won't be able to  challenge your pecs and shoulders all  that much because you'll give up before  you get to that point the solution is to  start the set on your fingers and simply  switch to regular push-ups once that  becomes too uncomfortable as for cardio  I like to use assistance cardio  movements to keep the pressure on the  same area so if I just did that shoulder  workout of some sort and I end by doing  a Tabata battle-ready protocol this  sends all the blood to the area just  trained helping it to recover and grow  and also building a huge insane pump  then there's the option of breaking your  workouts up throughout the day which  I've also talked about very recently and  this lends itself perfectly to this kind  of training if you have a six hour gap  between the heavy squat and the pistol  squat then you go be able to perform the  pistol squats without dealing with  Apetit from the heavy squat that way you  filt strength control balance and  endurance and more then in the evening  you might be some kettlebell swings for  endurance and pump this is also where  the incidental training can come in  training things throughout the day for  instance I might take five minutes  whilst my computer loads to perform some  handstand holds against the door seeing  as my main goal is the prior section and  balance that little bits of greasing the  groove is enough and it would  my shoulder strength for further  workouts later in the day this regular  movement also helps to keep my energy  levels up as I discussed recently when  it comes to selecting the movements  choose those that offer maximum bang for  the buck  those are movements that provide  multiple benefits rather than just one  hitting the heavy bag for instance is a  great form of cardio as is the rowing  machine but the heavy bag will let you  work on your technique and even increase  your mobility if you're throwing kicks  too it's up to you if you want to use  this with full body training bro splits  or push-pull legs but lately I've been  experimenting with what I call prime  focus workouts  these are full-body routines but with a  little extra emphasis on one particular  body part attribute or skill so I might  do a lot for the pecs but still hit  every other muscle group gently  throughout the rest of the day hopefully  that all makes sense if it sounds a bit  complicated then of course it can check  out my ebook and training program super  functional training which provides a  detailed routine that works just like  the one I've described here with a lot  more explanation as to how all that  works I also explained how you can cycle  between different training styles  without losing your gains and build up  the resilience you need by strengthening  connective tissue first this is a better  way to start out to many people seeing  as it's much easier to regain attributes  later on alternatively take a moment to  think about the attributes you would  like to Train whether that means finger  strength rotational strength max  strength endurance balance memory or all  of those things together then look into  the type of training you need to do to  improve in each one of those areas and  then swap them in or bolt them on to a  workout as appropriate this is a modular  approach and it allow Dee to pick and  choose the kind of things you want to  train so I hope you found this video  useful interesting guys if you did then  please leave a like comment subscribe  all that stuff helped me out I'd love to  know what tricks you've found to  incorporate lots of different training  methods all into a single program and  yeah I'll see you next time bye for now
 

